  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn upper level low will depart the region tonight. With the loss\nof daytime instability any showers should taper off this\nevening. Clouds will clear out with light winds overnight\nleading to fog formation. Lows tonight will fall into the 50's\nwith a warming trend starting Wednesday as highs reach 80. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nHigh pressure builds into the region for the middle of the week.\nTiming of the next system still looks to be in the Thursday to\nFriday timeframe. Enough instability and moisture looks present\nahead of the main cold front for a round or two of showers and \nthunderstorms. At this time it appears two clusters get going \nThursday afternoon one over NEPA/Sullivan NY as terrain \ninteracts with moisture advection and the second ahead of the \ncold front where lift is a bit more favorable. \n\nDepending on cloud cover the modeled environment still shows \nsome potential for strong to severe thunderstorms. Modeled CAPE\nranges from 750-1500 J/KG with around 30 knots of 0-6KM bulk\nshear Thursday afternoon and evening. Soundings do show some\nmid-level dry air and potential gusty wind concern. Ensemble \ndata is trending toward a slower frontal passage in \nNEPA/Sullivan NY Friday lingering the chances for showers and \nthunderstorms a little longer. High pressure is still expected\nfor the weekend. Highs still look be around 80 most days with\nThursday and Sunday the warmest days with more typical overnight\nlows as well in the 50's and low 60's.",
